          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What blood pressure target does guidance describe for most adults?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve does not give a single blood pressure target for most adults, and I can't fill that gap from memory.\n\n**What the retrieved guidance does say:**\n- **Classification (US society guidance, \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\"):** Normal is below 120/80 mm Hg. Elevated is 120 to 129 systolic with diastolic below 80. Stage 1 is 130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic. Stage 2 is 140 or higher systolic, or 90 or higher diastolic. This note says it covers \"the treatment target most bodies set,\" but the passage I received does not state that number.\n- **A target for people with coronary artery disease (\"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention,\" major cardiology society guidance):** Blood pressure below 130/80 mm Hg \"for most people.\" This applies to people already diagnosed with coronary disease. It is not a general target for all adults.\n\n**Where authorities disagree:**\n- The diagnosis note says US guidance starts hypertension at 130/80 mm Hg, while European guidance starts it at 140/90 mm Hg and treats 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as \"high-normal.\" The same reading can therefore be labeled differently, and the number of adults counted as hypertensive changes a lot between the two.\n- The treatment note says that because of this threshold gap, many people US guidance calls stage 1 are advised on lifestyle changes alone in European guidance.\n\n**How strong the evidence is:** The retrieved passages do not grade the trial evidence behind any blood pressure target, so I can't say how strong it is.\n\nBecause the general-adult target is missing from what I retrieved, a clinician or the full source document is the best next step. Bring your own readings and which guideline you're comparing against, since the threshold you're measured against depends on the country and guideline used.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
